\par\par \b \uc2  Baltic sentiment seen weaker on Russia turmoil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Leff \par \uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 1999\par 15:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  VILNIUS, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Russia's latest political upheaval may further weaken poor market sentiment in its Baltic neighbours, although their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  face no immediate threat, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The events in Russia do not add to the picture of stability. They clearly show that the crisis in Russia has not ended, as some analysts had said," said Hermis Finance chief analyst Nerijus Dagilis after Russian President Boris Yeltsin sacked his prime miniser. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin sacked Sergei Stepashin and appointed Security Council chief Vladimir Putin as acting premier, saying he wanted Putin to succeed him in the presidency. \par \par \uc2  The rouble weakened and Moscow shares fell on the surprise announcement of Russia's fifth new acting premier in 17 months. \par \par \uc2  But Baltic equity markets, like other international markets, took the news in their stride and ended mixed with no sharp movements in share indices and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets fairly quiet. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania, which came under pressure following last August's financial crisis in Moscow, had felt no speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  "Most banks have minimum exposure to Russia so it is really a matter of how the Baltic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen. Right now activity has not picked up," said Estonian Hansapank money trader Maivi Hanson. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the news was likely to hit Latvia's lat hardest since it has spent much of recent weeks stuck at the bottom of its one percent fluctuation band against the IMF's SDR world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Budget worries and then a weak dollar have spurred local demand for the greenback, causing a spate of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "For the lat this, of course, is not good news," said one Latvian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. \par \par \uc2  In Lithuania, where the litas is pegged at four to one dollar, analysts said there was greater concern that the government could press for a devaluation to boost growth than of a speculative attack causing a break to the peg. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Equities ended little changed, with Estonia's TALSE\tab    index down 0.15 percent, Latvia's RICI\tab    off 0.79 percent and Lithuania's Litin-10\tab \tab up 0.3 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Investors have already discounted Russia into prices. Instability is the normal situation there," said Optiva Pank equity dealer Kaur Elviste.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Baltic share markets, which have been punished by recurring Russian and emerging markets crises in the past two years, are not seen having room to fall much further. But the reshuffle could postpone indefinitely the return of foreign investors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (( Vilnius newsroom, +370 2 223 517, fax 223 514, reuters@reuters.lt )).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8902vtk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
