\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-Baltics feel the  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  strain. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1999\par 17:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Baltic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs have the technical strength to withstand recent devaluation pressures, foreign analysts say, but the question is whether governments are prepared to take the pain of maintaining them. \par \par \uc2  While few doubt Estonia's commitment to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, analysts say Lithuania's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg looks vulnerable to political adjustment, and Latvia's could follow suit. \par \par \uc2  "None of them will be forced into devaluation - they've got enough reserves," said Tim Ash, emerging Europe economist at Westdeutsche Landesbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But there is a real risk, particularly in Lithuania, that the government will decide the price of defending the peg by tightening fiscal policy is too much for the economy to bear." \par \par \uc2  Lithuanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Reinoldijus Sharkinas has long insisted that devaluing the litas from its current four-to-one dollar peg is out of the question. \par \par \uc2  Latvia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry have both said they see no devaluation threat to the lat, which trades in a band of plus or minus one percent around the International Monetary Fund's SDR basket \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as long as the government sticks to tough spending cuts this year. \par \par \uc2  Estonia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, which pegs the kroon at 15.6466 to the euro, is enshrined in the country's constitution. \par \par \uc2  But recession and big current-account deficits have weighed heavily on Baltic markets since Russia's financial crisis last year hit exports from the three former Soviet states, and a round of regional devaluations sapped their competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Worst hit are Lithuania and Latvia, which have not forged the close Scandinavian links that have buoyed the Estonian economy since independence in 1991. \par \par \uc2  Lithuania's current-account gap rose to a huge 12.1 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) last year, and to 9.6 percent in the first quarter of 1999. Latvia's was scarcely better, rising to 11 percent last year and 8.7 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Estonia's current-account deficit shrank to 8.6 percent of GDP last year, and 7.0 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Speculation rumbles on that Lithuania may take advantage of its planned switch from a dollar to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket peg, probably next year, to haul down the value of the litas, and analysts say the uncertainty is undermining equity prices. \par \par \uc2  Rating agency Fitch IBCA recently voiced "increasing concern about the appropriateness of the current level" of the litas. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  five days running to keep the lat within its trading band. \par \par \uc2  "Basically you have to look at the resolve on the part of politicians to hold the line, and in Lithuania it's weakest," Ash at WestLB said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's relentless rise for most of this year against the euro has piled on the pain for Lithuania, while easing pressure on Estonia and leaving the lat largely untouched. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the euro's recent upturn against the greenback offered Lithuania only limited relief. \par \par \uc2  "It gives them a bit more room, but fundamentally it doesn't change the current-account deficit," said Juliet Sampson, emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "They haven't reached breaking point, but that doesn't mean they won't choose to go." \par \par \uc2  Devaluation for one Baltic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would inevitably have a knock-on effect on the others, and could rattle other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs even further afield. \par \par \uc2  "If one goes it'll put a strain on all the others," Sampson said. "For that matter, if one goes it'll put a strain on Hong Kong to some extent." ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, gill.tudor@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv83037cf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
